{
  "_about": "Desk styles from the Product Designer (design-drafts/desk-textures/spec.md, final 2026-11-08). Texture is the owner's Grok grain; in Light and Dark the colour is the MA palette's. In Zen (the dusk entries; ADR 0093 and its addendum, build_zen.py, 2026-11-10) a tile is wood at low chroma, the one slightly warm thing in a cool slate office (the owner: \"I hate brown\"): a greyed light oak, a pale greyed ash, a dark greyed walnut, a cool grey linoleum. Zen desks sit on the Zen ground #4D5463.",
  "default": "oak",
  "order": [
    "oak",
    "ash",
    "walnut",
    "linoleum"
  ],
  "scale": {
    "tilePx": 1024,
    "deskUnits": 512,
    "pxPerUnit": 2,
    "why": "One tile covers 512 desk units, so a 600-unit desk shows at most one repeat and the grain keeps the size the stills were judged at. Above 2 px per unit the image is magnified, which a soft grain hides. Anchor at the desk top-left, offset by a hash of the desk id mod 512 (spec, Filling the panel)."
  },
  "etchNotes": {
    "light": "light letters: fill lighter than the desk; wall is a 1 px darker line above each stroke; no highlight",
    "dusk": "Zen: medium letters in the wood's own hue, lighter than the desk inside 1.8 to 3:1 of the tile's mean; wall darker line above; no highlight",
    "dark": "dark letters: fill darker than the desk; the wall merges with the fill; the highlight (1 px lighter line below each stroke) is the line that reads, so fillVsGrooveLine is measured against highlight in Dark and against wall elsewhere"
  },
  "deskTextNotes": "deskText replaces text.secondary for text drawn on the desk (the strip status line). text.secondary fails 4.5:1 on mid-tone desks, and mid-tone desks are what the owner's etch rule needs (light letters must be lighter than the desk). In Zen only walnut holds text.secondary; linoleum takes text.primary, and oak and ash a cool near-black. moreContrast is for prefers-contrast: more.",
  "darkTiles": "In Dark, resting app tiles on a textured desk wear a 1 px rgba(255,255,255,0.12) edge (elevation.tileStacked).",
